Protocol: PhotoPrint_W2

Types:
    Agent A, B, P;
    Number grant, photos;

Knowledge:
    A: A, B, P, pk(B), pk(P);
    B: A, B, P, pk(B), inv(pk(B)), pk(P);
    P: A, B, P, pk(P), inv(pk(P)), pk(B)

Actions:
    A -> P: {A, B, grant}pk(P)
    P -> B: {A, P, grant}pk(B)
    B -> P: {photos}pk(P)

Goals:
    grant secret between A, P;
    photos secret between B, P;



# Special Task - Week 2: Static Analysis (Dolev-Yao)

# Intruder's initial knowledge:
#   A, P, B, pk(A), pk(B), pk(P), inv(pk(i))

# Messages observed on the network:
#   msg1 = {A, B, grant}pk(P)       (sent by A to P)
#   msg2 = {A, P, grant}pk(B)       (sent by P to B)
#   msg3 = {photos}pk(P)            (sent by B to P)

# What can the intruder learn?

#   - msg1 is encrypted with pk(P). The intruder does not have
#     inv(pk(P)), so it cannot decrypt msg1.
#     => grant and the authorization details remain hidden.

#   - msg2 is encrypted with pk(B). The intruder does not have
#     inv(pk(B)), so it cannot decrypt msg2.
#     => grant remains hidden.

#   - msg3 is encrypted with pk(P). The intruder does not have
#     inv(pk(P)), so it cannot decrypt msg3.
#     => photos remain hidden.

# Conclusion:
#   The intruder learns nothing beyond its initial knowledge.
#   Specifically, it cannot learn 'grant' or 'photos'.
#   The secrecy goals hold under passive attack.